---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site.config';
import { TOOLS, latestReviewDate } from '../data/tools';
import { CATEGORIES } from '../data/categories';
import { formatDate } from '../lib/dates';

const toolCount = TOOLS.length;
const categoryCount = CATEGORIES.length;
---

<Base
  title={`About ${SITE.name}`}
  description={`Who builds ${SITE.name}, how the tools are tested, how the pages are written, how the site is funded, and how to report a bug.`}
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'About' }]} />
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About {SITE.name}</h1>
    <p>
      {SITE.name} is a collection of {toolCount} single-purpose text and data tools in {categoryCount} categories, all of
      which run in your browser. Each one does a chore you could script in a minute but would rather not script again:
      joining a column into a comma list, reading the claims inside a JWT, finding the line where a JSON payload breaks.
    </p>

    <h2 id="who">Who builds it</h2>
    <p>
      I'm <strong>{SITE.author}</strong>{SITE.linkedin && <> (<a href={SITE.linkedin} rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>)</>}, and I
      build and maintain everything here: the conversion engines, their tests, the pages and the hosting. I work as a
      backend-focused software developer, which is exactly why these tools exist — most of them started as small scripts I
      kept rewriting for myself before I put them behind a UI. The site started on {formatDate(SITE.launched)} as two
      converters, column-to-list and list-to-column, that I wanted in a form I could bookmark, and has grown one tool at a
      time since. There is no team and no company behind it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="why">Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      Most "online converter" pages send what you paste to a server, wrap the result in a page of filler, and stop at the
      easy cases. I wanted the opposite: a page that does the conversion on your own device, tells you exactly what it
      changed (the counters and status lines on every tool), and documents the awkward inputs — quotes inside values,
      tokens with the wrong number of segments, integers too large for JSON — because those are the cases you actually
      search for.
    </p>

    <h2 id="testing">How the tools are tested</h2>
    <p>
      Every tool is split into a pure conversion engine with no browser code and a thin layer that wires it to the page.
      The engines have unit tests that run before each build; a test also checks the tool registry itself, so a page
      cannot ship with an over-long title, a duplicate search intent, or a missing review date. The worked example on
      each tool page is produced by running that tool's real engine at build time, not typed in, so the documentation
      cannot drift from what the tool does.
    </p>

    <h2 id="writing">How the pages are written</h2>
    <p>
      The text on a tool page comes from running the tool against real and deliberately broken input and writing down
      what happens. I sometimes use writing tools to draft or tidy prose, but every sentence is checked against the
      running tool and edited by me before it is published, and each page shows the date I last did that review. If you
      find a claim on a page that the tool does not live up to, that is a bug — please tell me.
    </p>

    <h2 id="privacy">What happens to your data</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing you paste is uploaded, and not as a policy choice you have to trust: the site is a set of static files with
      no server component, so there is nowhere for the text to go. You can load a tool, disconnect from the internet, and
      keep using it. The <a href="/privacy-policy">privacy policy</a> covers the two third-party services that do run on
      the page, advertising and analytics, and what they can see.
    </p>

    <h2 id="funding">How the site is funded</h2>
    <p>
      The plan is to cover hosting and development with display advertising through Google AdSense — the account is
      currently under review, so no ads are running yet and I'm covering costs myself in the meantime. Once approved,
      ads will never appear above a tool or between its input and output, there will be at most two on a page, and the
      tools will work identically with an ad blocker on. Text you paste into a tool is never used for advertising,
      because it never leaves the page.
    </p>

    <h2 id="bugs">Reporting a bug or requesting a tool</h2>
    <p>
      {SITE.github ? (
        <>
          The source code is public at <a href={SITE.github} rel="noopener">GitHub</a> — open an
          {' '}<a href={`${SITE.github}/issues`} rel="noopener">issue</a> with the tool name, the browser you used, a small
          sample of the input and what you expected. I read everything and usually reply within a few working days.
        </>
      ) : (
        <>See the <a href="/contact">contact page</a> to report a bug or suggest a tool.</>
      )}
    </p>
    <p class="muted">Last content review across the site: {formatDate(latestReviewDate())}.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
